# Node.js/Express Security Checklist

## 🔴 Authentication & Authorization
- [ ] Implement authentication middleware
- [ ] Use JWT tokens or session-based auth
- [ ] Implement proper password hashing (bcrypt, Argon2)
- [ ] Add API key validation for service-to-service calls
- [ ] Implement role-based access control (RBAC)
- [ ] Use OAuth2/OpenID Connect for external auth
- [ ] Implement proper session management
- [ ] Add logout functionality with token invalidation

## 🔴 Input Validation & Sanitization
- [ ] Validate all user inputs (body, query, params)
- [ ] Sanitize inputs to prevent XSS
- [ ] Use parameterized queries for database operations
- [ ] Implement request size limits
- [ ] Validate file uploads (type, size, content)
- [ ] Use CSRF tokens for state-changing operations
- [ ] Implement content-type validation

## 🔴 Security Headers & CORS
- [ ] Install and configure Helmet middleware
- [ ] Set Content-Security-Policy header
- [ ] Configure restrictive CORS policy
- [ ] Implement HSTS for HTTPS enforcement
- [ ] Set X-Frame-Options to DENY
- [ ] Set X-Content-Type-Options to nosniff
- [ ] Set Referrer-Policy appropriately

## 🔴 Rate Limiting & DoS Protection
- [ ] Implement rate limiting on all endpoints
- [ ] Add IP-based blocking for abusive clients
- [ ] Implement request timeout handling
- [ ] Add payload size limits
- [ ] Use CDN/WAF for DDoS protection
- [ ] Implement circuit breakers for external services

## 🔴 Dependency Security
- [ ] Regularly run `npm audit`
- [ ] Use `npm audit fix` to update vulnerable dependencies
- [ ] Implement automated dependency updates
- [ ] Use lockfiles (package-lock.json, yarn.lock)
- [ ] Review third-party package permissions
- [ ] Monitor for dependency confusion attacks

## 🔴 Secrets Management
- [ ] Never commit secrets to version control
- [ ] Use environment variables for configuration
- [ ] Implement proper .gitignore for sensitive files
- [ ] Use secret management services (AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault)
- [ ] Rotate secrets regularly
- [ ] Implement canary tokens for detection

## 🟡 Error Handling & Logging
- [ ] Implement centralized error handling
- [ ] Don't expose stack traces in production
- [ ] Use structured logging
- [ ] Avoid logging sensitive data
- [ ] Implement log rotation and retention policies
- [ ] Monitor logs for security events
- [ ] Use different log levels appropriately

## 🟡 Database Security
- [ ] Use connection pooling
- [ ] Implement query timeouts
- [ ] Use read-only users where possible
- [ ] Regular database backups
- [ ] Encrypt sensitive data at rest
- [ ] Implement database activity monitoring
- [ ] Use TLS for database connections

## 🟡 API Security
- [ ] Implement API versioning
- [ ] Add request/response validation
- [ ] Implement proper HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE)
- [ ] Use appropriate HTTP status codes
- [ ] Implement API documentation with security requirements
- [ ] Add health check endpoints
- [ ] Implement graceful degradation

## 🟢 Development & Deployment
- [ ] Use HTTPS in production
- [ ] Implement security headers in reverse proxy
- [ ] Regular security training for developers
- [ ] Implement pre-commit hooks for security checks
- [ ] Use SAST (Static Application Security Testing) tools
- [ ] Implement DAST (Dynamic Application Security Testing)
- [ ] Regular penetration testing
- [ ] Security code reviews

## 🟢 Monitoring & Incident Response
- [ ] Implement security monitoring
- [ ] Set up alerts for suspicious activity
- [ ] Create incident response plan
- [ ] Regular security audits
- [ ] Implement anomaly detection
- [ ] Monitor canary tokens
- [ ] Track security metrics

## Quick Fixes for Current App

### Immediate Actions:
1. **Create package.json**:
```bash
npm init -y
npm install express helmet cors express-rate-limit
```

2. **Update .gitignore**:
```
.env*
node_modules/
*.log
*.pid
.DS_Store
```

